  "llm_reasoning_text": "\n## Index Tuning Summary\n\n### Strategy\nThis is a Star Schema Benchmark (SSB) workload with a large `lineorder` fact table joined to four dimension tables. The dominant costs are:\n1. Full scans of `lineorder` (~196K cost) for every query\n2. Sequential scans of dimension tables with selective filters\n\n### Key Indexes and Their Impact\n\n---\n\n#### 1. `lineorder(lo_orderdate, lo_discount, lo_quantity, lo_extendedprice)` \u2014 **Critical for Q1.x**\n- Enables index-only scans for Q1.1/Q1.2/Q1.3 which filter on `lo_discount` and `lo_quantity` ranges and join to `date_dim` on `lo_orderdate`\n- Cost reduction: Q1.1: 217K \u2192 187K, Q1.2: 222K \u2192 **9.5K**, Q1.3: 222K \u2192 **2.3K** (100x improvement)\n- The leading column `lo_orderdate` allows the planner to do nested-loop index lookups per date key, then the discount/quantity predicates filter within those, and `lo_extendedprice` makes it a covering index-only scan\n\n---\n\n#### 2. `lineorder(lo_partkey, lo_suppkey, lo_custkey, lo_orderdate, lo_revenue, lo_supplycost)` \u2014 **Critical for Q2.x, Q3.x, Q4.x**\n- This single wide covering index supports all join patterns across Q2, Q3, and Q4 groups by leading with `lo_partkey` (used in Q2.x, Q4.x) and including `lo_suppkey`/`lo_custkey` (used in Q3.x, Q4.x)\n- The planner can do nested-loop index-only scans: for each filtered part/supplier/customer row, look up matching lineorder rows by the FK key\n- Cost reductions observed:\n  - Q2.1: 211K \u2192 **195K**; Q2.2: 205K \u2192 **13.9K**; Q2.3: 205K \u2192 **13.6K** (huge for selective brand filters)\n  - Q3.3: 204K \u2192 **48.5K**; Q3.4: 204K \u2192 **48.6K** (city filters very selective)\n  - Q4.3: 207K \u2192 **202K** (moderate improvement; customer filter limits benefit)\n- `lo_supplycost` included as a covering column for Q4.x profit = `lo_revenue - lo_supplycost`\n\n---\n\n#### 3. `date_dim(d_year)`, `date_dim(d_yearmonthnum)`, `date_dim(d_yearmonth)`, `date_dim(d_year, d_weeknuminyear)` \u2014 **Dimension filter indexes**\n- `d_year`: Used in Q1.1, Q3.1-Q3.3, allows index scan to quickly fetch the 365 rows for a given year instead of seq scan\n- `d_yearmonthnum`: Used in Q1.2, retrieves ~31 rows for a specific year-month\n- `d_year + d_weeknuminyear`: Used in Q1.3, retrieves ~7 rows for a specific week of a year  \n- `d_yearmonth`: Used in Q3.4, retrieves ~31 rows for a named month like 'Dec1997'\n- `date_dim` is small enough that seq scans are often chosen for range predicates; these indexes help the nested-loop join path in Q1.x\n\n---\n\n#### 4. `part(p_category)`, `part(p_brand)`, `part(p_mfgr, p_partkey)` \u2014 **Part dimension filters**\n- `p_category`: Used in Q2.1, Q4.3; bitmap index scan for ~5K/4.7K matching rows\n- `p_brand`: Used in Q2.2 (range), Q2.3 (equality); dramatically reduces probe set\n- `p_mfgr + p_partkey`: Used in Q4.1/Q4.2 where `p_mfgr IN ('MFGR#1','MFGR#2')` matches ~50% of parts. The covering `p_partkey` column enables an index-only scan that avoids heap access. Q4.1 cost improved from 212K to **210K**.\n\n---\n\n#### 5. `supplier(s_region)`, `supplier(s_nation)` \u2014 **Supplier dimension filters**\n- Supplier is a small table (~2000 rows) so seq scans are common; these bitmap indexes help when selectivity is high\n- `s_region`: Used in Q2.1-Q2.3, Q3.1, Q4.1-Q4.3 (filters to ~1/5 of rows)\n- `s_nation`: Used in Q3.2, Q4.3 (filters to ~1/25 of rows, very selective)\n- The planner selectively uses these vs seq scans based on join ordering\n\n---\n\n#### 6. `customer(c_region, c_custkey, c_nation)` \u2014 **Covering index for region-based customer lookup**\n- `c_region` filter (Q3.1, Q4.1-Q4.3) reduces customer set to ~6K rows (~20% selectivity)\n- Including `c_custkey` and `c_nation` makes this a covering index-only scan \u2014 no heap access needed\n- Q4.1 confirmed using this as an index-only scan: 212K \u2192 **211.7K**; Q3.1: 209K \u2192 **209.2K**\n\n#### 7. `customer(c_nation)` \u2014 **Nation filter**\n- `c_nation` filter in Q3.2 reduces customer to ~1260 rows (~5% selectivity)\n- Confirmed beneficial via bitmap heap scan\n\n#### 8. `customer(c_city, c_custkey)` \u2014 **City filter with covering key**\n- `c_city IN (...)` in Q3.3/Q3.4 is very selective (~260 rows out of 30K)\n- Including `c_custkey` enables index-only scan: Q3.3 confirmed using this to resolve customer join efficiently\n\n### Final Cost Summary (representative queries)\n| Query | Baseline | With Indexes | Improvement |\n|-------|----------|--------------|-------------|\n| Q1.1 | ~217K | ~187K | 14% |\n| Q1.2 | ~222K | ~9.5K | 96% |\n| Q1.3 | ~222K | ~2.3K | 99% |\n| Q2.1 | ~211K | ~195K | 8% |\n| Q2.2 | ~205K | ~13.9K | 93% |\n| Q2.3 | ~205K | ~13.6K | 93% |\n| Q3.3 | ~204K | ~48.5K | 76% |\n| Q3.4 | ~204K | ~48.6K | 76% |\n| Q4.3 | ~207K | ~202K | 2% |\n",
